e'll get to hear a lot about the impact  of higher inflation um whether this  hiring trend is is that slowed down  considerably is continuing or if things  are starting to take a turn around a  corner so that's going to be big i think  on wednesday definitely take a look at  that i also want to talk about the major  themes in the markets for stocks and for  bonds because i think we've got two  very different kinds of markets there  and what i'm hearing from investors and  the folks on wall street that i talk to  about both the stock and the bond market  so want to take a look you know you're  you're with us on the live stream thank  you so much for doing that and for being  with us uh we're going to be doing this  every monday right now going forward at  1 pm eastern so appreciate everyone who  tuned in  when i look out at the stock market  right when you talk about the sell-off  last week and the sell-off the week  before last week at least there wasn't  really a major catalyst for that  sell-off yeah we got that cpi number the  inflation number 8.5 percent the highest  in 40 years again right the month over  month number  um was high but when you looked at that  core numbers and when you take out food  and energy prices which are historically  volatile uh which tend to jump up a lot  more than the rest of the index that  number was only 0.3 percent and that got  a lot of folks talking about the  possibility that we've peaked on  inflation that this last month we got in  march 8.5 percent increase year over  year on the cpi consumer price index  may have been the highest we get and now  we start to gradually decline that was  sort of the the rebound or the reaction  i got that i heard from folks i know on  wall street um  you know when i talked to annetta  markowska she's the chief economist at  td securities she says yeah this is this  the numbers in this report suggest that  we have peaked and now we're starting to  come down we should start to come down  on infl